  "tldr": "Average-case sensitivity replaces a worst-case propensity-ratio cap by a calibratable second moment, but its published sharpness and inference theory is cross-sectional. For two observational treatment decisions and the fixed regime \\((1,1)\\), this proposal characterizes the entire compatible counterfactual-mean set by a weakly compact perspective program, restores the early ratio's required \\((L_0,Y)\\) factorization by conditional projection, constructs endpoint-attaining full laws, and derives a two-global-price conditional clipped-affine dual on active and boundary faces. The statistical kernel is an optimizer-aware efficient score with both propensity-floor corrections and a primitive-rate proof of uniform quadratic held-out bias, yielding a joint multiplier band on regular budget regions. Range extension and monotone set inversion then provide honest full-ray breakdown confidence sets at ties, flat crossings, absent crossings, and excluded nonregular faces.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Zhang and Zhao solve the one-treatment second-moment model and explicitly name longitudinal observations as future work; Tan solves the compatible longitudinal full-law problem for pointwise ratio bounds but leaves specialized computation and sample inference open. The first missing identification step is a history-respecting projection under a transported perspective budget, and the first missing inference step is stability of nested conditional roots and two global prices when both clipping floors are estimated.",
